{"text": " The target isn't three million barrels of oil, it's three million barrels of oil equivalent, which means natural gas counts. Bessent said, we're going to grow our way out of this. The strategy he laid out for achieving this is called three-three-three. Three percent real GDP growth, the federal deficit cut to three percent of GDP, and three million more barrels of oil a day, or the equivalent from other energy sources. On crude alone, this isn't close. Crude output is already at record levels, and forecasts estimate marginal gains at best. But natural gas is also at an all-time high, and it's climbed almost every year for the better part of a decade. Stack the two together, and the combined number is set to clear the target. So Target One clears, right up until you check where the bar was set. Over Biden's four years, American oil and gas production rose about four and a half million barrels a day of oil equivalent. Over Trump's first term, before the pandemic, in under three years, It rose about eight and a half million. The target for Bessin's master plan to fix the economy is three million. So the goal was never ambitious. It would be like if Shaq set a goal to dunk on an eight-foot basketball hoop. Bessin picked a target for energy that was going to be hit whether anybody did anything or not.", "segments": [{"id": 0, "seek": 0, "start": 0.0, "end": 2.84, "text": " The target isn't three million barrels of oil,", "tokens": [50363, 383, 2496, 2125, 470, 1115, 1510, 17907, 286, 3056, 11, 50513], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.23615768878129276, "compression_ratio": 1.7785714285714285, "no_speech_prob": 0.006843588314950466, "words": [{"word": " The", "start": 0.0, "end": 0.28, "probability": 0.36501410603523254}, {"word": " target", "start": 0.28, "end": 0.54, "probability": 0.9578736424446106}, {"word": " isn't", "start": 0.54, "end": 0.94, "probability": 0.9658538997173309}, {"word": " three", "start": 0.94, "end": 1.26, "probability": 0.4856774